From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Faust <willtechnoduck@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 00:45:53 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Read the TEB from the TSD slot wine owns, not from hardcoded
 slot 275

Every TEB recovery on iOS goes through TPIDRRO_EL0 plus a raw Apple TSD slot,
since x18 does not survive context switches. That slot was the constant 0x898
(slot 275) in seven places: IOS_TEB_TSD_OFFSET feeding six JIT emit sites,
the IOS_LOAD_TEB macro in Module.S, and two direct reads in Module.cpp.

275 is a DYNAMIC pthread key. Nobody guaranteed it to us; wine simply wrote
the TEB there and it worked wherever nothing else had claimed it yet. On an
M4 iPad the real owner turned up -- something in the Metal stack, brought up
by the first nextDrawable -- reclaimed the key and reset the slot to NULL.
Every one of these reads then produced TEB=0, and the process died
dereferencing TEB->PEB at address 0x60, 49 c0000005 deep, in a redelivery
storm that terminated it.

The slot is not a constant. Four values have been seen on real devices: 275,
276, 280 (M4 iPad / iOS 26.4), 284 (iPhone 13 Pro / iOS 27). The iPhone's own
key was 284 the whole time -- wine's TEB lived in a slot it did not own while
the slot it did own sat unused, so that device was one framework load away
from the same failure.

IosTebTsdOffset is imported once in ProcessInit from wine's ntdll data export
and read by the six JIT emitters at emit time. A missing or zero export is
fatal, not a reason to fall back: the constant is precisely the value that is
wrong, and a bad offset does not fail here, it fails arbitrarily far away in
whichever thread next touches a TEB.

Module.S keeps 0x898 as a placeholder. Its three-instruction triplet is
recognised and rewritten by wine's x18 binary patcher through the .text RW
alias before the code is ever executed, which avoids giving the hottest EC
thunks a scratch register they do not have -- at check_target_ec the stack is
8-mod-16 after the return-address push, so a spill there would fault on SP
alignment. The comment there says not to "correct" the constant and not to
reorder the triplet, because an unmatched sequence would silently keep
reading the wrong slot.

Verified on both regimes with the offset genuinely differing from the old
constant, so this exercised the new path rather than coinciding with it:
iPhone 13 Pro / iOS 27 offset 0x8e0, M4 iPad / iOS 26.4 offset 0x8c0. Both
report "found 6 static TSD read(s), retargeted 6" -- matching an offline scan
of the shipped .text -- and run cube-x64 with zero faults. The M4 went from
49 c0000005 and termination to 896 frames at 16.5ms.

IosTebTsdImportFound is uint32_t rather than bool: as a one-byte global it
misaligned the adrp/ldr pair lld generates for a neighbouring word
("misaligned ldr/str offset").

